# What is Rocci?

**Prerequisites:** none beyond the [docs prerequisites](/docs/start/prerequisites/).
**You will learn:** what a `.rocci` file is, what Rocci generates, and when not to use it.
**Time:** about five minutes of reading.
**Next:** [Choose your path](/docs/start/choose-your-path/).

A `.rocci` module is mostly Roc. `@component` marks the regions that use a
bounded HTML template grammar. The compiler lowers those regions to ordinary
Roc calls such as `Html.element`, `Html.attribute`, and `Html.text`. Roc
type-checking starts after that lowering. Rocci does not replace Roc.

## Supported shapes

| Shape | What you author | What `rocci run` does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Component module | `@component`, `@css`, `@fixture` | Preview with `rocci view` / `rocci browse` |
| Standalone app | `@context`, `@init`, `@view`, optional `@patch` / `@command` / `@live` | Generate a server and serve it |
| Custom app | `main.roc` plus `.rocci` modules | Compile sibling modules and start your Roc program |
| Desktop bundle | `rocci.toml` plus an app entry | Wrap the server in an ad-hoc macOS `.app` (`rocci bundle`) |

## Compile-to-Roc

Rocci is a compiler input, not a second runtime component system. You keep
writing Roc for types, helpers, SQLite, and effects. You write Rocci where
markup, scoped CSS, fixtures, and HTTP declarations are the shorter spelling.

Datastar is the **browser transport**: it sends intent and morphs HTML. It is
not a client application framework and not a Rocci language feature.

## Deliberate limits

- Not JSX embedded throughout Roc.
- No runtime component registry and no magic `children` field.
- No markup inside arbitrary Roc expressions; use `@if` / `@for` / `@match`.
- No dynamic tag names or prop spreading.
- Rocdown is a [separate content product](/rocdown/), not a fourth piece of Rocci.

Continue with [choose your path](/docs/start/choose-your-path/) or jump to
[Rocci in five minutes](/docs/start/five-minutes/) if the toolchain is already
installed.
